{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Where the baseline pattern can still go wrong</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Don't reach for disabling a whole rule (<code>rules: { 'color-contrast': { enabled: false } }</code>) to make noisy pages quiet, that turns the rule off everywhere, including on new code. Allowlist the specific known instances instead, so new violations of the same rule elsewhere still get caught.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Dynamic content needs care: run axe against a stable DOM state (after loading spinners resolve, after animations settle) or results will vary run to run and either flake CI or silently miss real elements. For third-party embeds you don't control (an ad iframe, a payment widget), use the <code>context.exclude</code> option to scope the scan away from that subtree rather than disabling the rules it happens to trigger sitewide.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Treat the baseline file itself as a tracked liability, not a one-time setup artifact: require a linked ticket for any addition, and put its total entry count on a dashboard so it trends toward zero rather than growing invisibly. A baseline with no burn-down discipline decays into the same "everything's allowed" state as the disabled check it replaced, just via a slower, quieter path that's easier to miss in review.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

